---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

/* --- Contact --------------------------------------------------------------
   dmca@bleepboard.com is the one address that demonstrably exists (it is the
   target of every Report menu on every sound page, and of /dmca/). It is used
   here as the working fallback so this page never points at a black hole.

   TODO (owner): provision `privacy@bleepboard.com` and swap PRIVACY_TO to it.
   Both AdSense and Mediavine expect a contact route on the privacy policy, and
   a shared legal/copyright inbox is workable but not ideal. Until the mailbox
   exists, do NOT print it — an address that bounces is worse than a shared one.
------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const PRIVACY_TO = 'dmca@bleepboard.com';

/* --- Ad status ------------------------------------------------------------
   THE SINGLE SWITCH FOR THIS PAGE.

   Bleepboard serves no ads today. Verified, not assumed: there is no AdSense,
   Mediavine, GPT or any other ad tag anywhere in src/ or public/, and a built
   dist/ contains no third-party script of any kind.

   The advertising section below is written to be truthful in BOTH states: it
   currently says ads are not served and describes what will change when they
   are. When ads actually go live, flip ADS_LIVE to true — that rewrites the
   status note and the tense-sensitive lines, and nothing else on the page
   needs touching.

   TODO (owner): before flipping this, see the "Before ads go live" checklist
   in the report — in particular, a Google-certified consent management
   platform must be serving EEA/UK traffic FIRST. Flipping this flag does not
   make the site compliant; it only makes this page describe reality.
------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const ADS_LIVE = false;

/* Opt-out routes. These are the controls the networks themselves point at.
   We link them; we do not operate them and cannot promise what they do. */
const optOuts = [
  {
    label: 'Google Ads Settings',
    href: 'https://myadcenter.google.com/',
    blurb:
      'Turn off personalised advertising across Google’s own products and the sites that use Google’s ad technology, including this one if it ever does.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Google — how ads work',
    href: 'https://policies.google.com/technologies/ads',
    blurb:
      'Google’s own description of the cookies and identifiers it uses to serve and measure ads. Worth reading before you decide what to switch off.',
  },
  {
    label: 'aboutads.info opt-out',
    href: 'https://optout.aboutads.info/',
    blurb:
      'The Digital Advertising Alliance’s industry page for opting out of interest-based ads from many third-party vendors at once.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Your Online Choices (EU/UK)',
    href: 'https://www.youronlinechoices.eu/',
    blurb:
      'The European equivalent, run by EDAA. Same idea, different set of participating vendors.',
  },
  {
    label: 'NAI opt-out',
    href: 'https://optout.networkadvertising.org/',
    blurb:
      'Network Advertising Initiative member opt-outs, covering another slice of the ad-tech supply chain.',
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Privacy Policy | Bleepboard"
  description="What Bleepboard does and does not collect: no accounts, no cookies, no analytics, and one favourites list that never leaves your browser. Plus how advertising, embeds and hosting logs work."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="tick"></span>Legal</p>
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Bleepboard is a static website that plays short audio clips. There is no account
      to make, no login, no newsletter and no database with your name in it. This page
      says exactly what happens to data when you use the site — including the parts
      that are genuinely nothing.
    </p>
    {/* TODO (owner): an effective / last-updated date belongs directly under the
        lede. Deliberately omitted rather than invented — a fabricated date on a
        privacy policy is a real problem, not a cosmetic one. Add it as static
        text (not `new Date()`, which would silently claim the policy changed on
        every deploy). */}
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 0. the short version ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="summary">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      The whole policy in a paragraph, for people who are here to play a sound and leave.
    </p>

    <div class="summary">
      <p class="summary-lead">
        Bleepboard does not ask you for personal information and does not set cookies.
        The only thing it saves is your favourites list, which is stored in your own
        browser and never sent to us. There are currently no analytics and no
        advertising on the site. The web host that serves the pages keeps standard
        access logs, as every web host does. If advertising is added later, ad
        companies will set their own cookies — that section is below, and it is
        clearly marked as not yet active.
      </p>

      <div class="ledger">
        <div class="ledger-col">
          <p class="ledger-head" data-kind="yes">What is stored</p>
          <ul class="ledger-list">
            <li>Your favourites, in your browser’s local storage</li>
            <li>Standard web-server access logs, held by the host</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ledger-col">
          <p class="ledger-head" data-kind="no">What is not</p>
          <ul class="ledger-list">
            <li>No accounts, names, emails or passwords</li>
            <li>No cookies set by this site</li>
            <li>No analytics or visitor tracking</li>
            <li>No advertising cookies — today</li>
            <li>No selling or sharing of personal information</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 1. on your device ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="device">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What the site stores on your device</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      One key, holding a list of sound names. That is the entirety of it.
    </p>

    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt>Storage key</dt>
        <dd><code>bleepboard:favourites</code></dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Mechanism</dt>
        <dd>Browser local storage — not a cookie, so it is never attached to a network request.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Contents</dt>
        <dd>A list of the short names of the sounds you saved, most recent first. Nothing else.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Where it goes</dt>
        <dd>Nowhere. It is read and written by code running in your browser and is never transmitted to us or anyone else.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Because it lives in your browser rather than on a server, your favourites are
        per-browser and per-device. Save something on your phone and it will not appear
        on your laptop. That is a genuine limitation, and it is the direct consequence
        of not having accounts.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your browser blocks site data — a private window, or strict privacy settings —
        the favourite button disables itself and says so, rather than accepting taps it
        cannot remember. The site also writes and immediately deletes a tiny throwaway
        value on load purely to test whether storage works at all; it holds nothing and
        does not survive the check.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>To clear it:</strong> use your browser’s “clear site data”, “cookies and
        site data”, or “clear browsing data” option for bleepboard.com. In most browsers
        that setting is under Settings → Privacy. Clearing it deletes your favourites
        permanently and immediately — there is no copy anywhere else to delete, and
        nothing for us to do on our end.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="origin-note">
      <b>Note</b>
      <span>
        Playing a sound also asks your device for a very short vibration on phones that
        support it, and the share and copy buttons hand text to your operating system’s
        share sheet or clipboard. Those are browser features acting on your device. We
        never see what you shared, or where you sent it.
      </span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 2. advertising ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="advertising">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Third-party advertising</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      The one part of this page that describes companies other than us.
    </p>

    {!ADS_LIVE && (
      <p class="status">
        <b>Not active</b>
        <span>
          Bleepboard is not currently showing ads, and no advertising code runs on this
          site today. This section describes what would apply if and when advertising is
          added, so that it is here to read before anything changes rather than after.
        </span>
      </p>
    )}
    {ADS_LIVE && (
      <p class="status" data-live>
        <b>Active</b>
        <span>
          Bleepboard currently shows advertising supplied by third-party ad networks. The
          following describes how that works and how to opt out.
        </span>
      </p>
    )}

    {/* TODO (owner): when ads go live, set ADS_LIVE = true at the top of this file.
        Also name the actual network(s) in the paragraph below — right now it says
        "Google AdSense and/or a managed provider such as Mediavine" because the
        decision has not been made. Once one is signed, say which one, and delete
        the other. Vague-but-honest is fine before launch; vague after launch is not. */}

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Bleepboard is free, and the plan for keeping it that way is advertising rather
        than paywalls or data sales. The intended route is Google AdSense and/or a
        managed advertising provider such as Mediavine. When advertising runs on a site,
        the following is true of essentially all of them, and would be true here:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Third-party vendors, including Google, use cookies and similar
          identifiers to serve ads</strong> based on your prior visits to this website
          and to other websites.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google’s use of advertising cookies</strong> enables it and its partners
          to serve ads to you based on your visits to Bleepboard and other sites on the
          internet. You can opt out of personalised advertising by visiting
          <a href="https://myadcenter.google.com/" rel="noopener">Google Ads Settings</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ad exchanges and demand partners</strong> sit behind the ad slot itself.
          A single ad placement can involve many companies bidding in real time, each of
          which may set or read its own identifier. That chain is operated by the ad
          network, not by us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You can opt out of third-party vendor cookies</strong> for interest-based
          advertising at <a href="https://optout.aboutads.info/" rel="noopener">aboutads.info</a>,
          and from the other industry pages listed below.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Advertising cookies are set by those companies under their own privacy policies,
        not ours. We do not receive, store or have access to the identifiers they use, we
        cannot read them, and we cannot delete them for you — the opt-out links below go
        directly to the parties that can. Nor can we make promises on their behalf about
        what they do with what they collect; the only accurate thing to say is that their
        policies govern it, and they are worth reading.
      </p>

      <h3>If you are in the EEA or the UK</h3>
      <p>
        Under EU and UK law, personalised advertising and the cookies behind it require
        your consent, given freely and before anything is set. Consent is collected
        through a consent management platform — the banner that asks what you allow — and
        your choice there governs whether personalised ads are shown to you and whether
        advertising cookies are placed.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Bleepboard does not currently operate a consent banner, because it
        currently serves no ads and sets no cookies of its own.</strong> One must be in
        place before advertising is switched on for visitors in those regions. We are
        saying that plainly rather than describing a mechanism that does not exist.
      </p>
      {/* TODO (owner) — REAL COMPLIANCE GAP, NOT A COPY PROBLEM:
          Google requires a Google-certified CMP for EEA/UK traffic on any property
          serving AdSense/AdMob ads, and both GDPR and the ePrivacy rules require
          prior consent for advertising cookies regardless of network. Serving
          personalised ads to EEA/UK visitors without a CMP is a policy violation and
          a legal exposure, not a nice-to-have.

          Mediavine ships a CMP as part of its script; AdSense alone does not — with
          bare AdSense you must add a certified CMP yourself.

          When a CMP exists, this subsection must be rewritten to describe it: which
          CMP, what it asks, and how a visitor reopens it to change their answer.
          Do not soften the paragraph above until that is genuinely true. */}
    </div>

    <div class="opt-grid">
      {optOuts.map((o) => (
        <a class="opt-card" href={o.href} rel="noopener">
          <b>{o.label}</b>
          <span class="opt-blurb">{o.blurb}</span>
          <span class="opt-host">{new URL(o.href).host}</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="origin-note">
      <b>Note</b>
      <span>
        These opt-outs are stored by your browser, which means clearing your site data
        can clear the opt-out along with everything else, and an opt-out set in one
        browser does not carry to another. That is how the industry built it, not a
        choice made here.
      </span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 3. analytics ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="analytics">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Analytics</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      A short section, because the honest answer is short.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <strong>There are none.</strong> Bleepboard runs no Google Analytics, no Tag
        Manager, no Plausible, Fathom or Umami, no heatmap or session-recording tool, and
        no pixel from any social network. No third-party script of any kind loads on
        these pages. The fonts are the ones already on your device, so not even a
        typeface is fetched from someone else’s server.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is one loose end worth being straight about. When you play a sound, the
        site fires a single tiny same-origin request intended to count plays, so that the
        trending list could reflect what people actually play. <strong>Nothing is
        listening at the other end.</strong> The counter it was written for was never
        connected, so the request reaches the static host, finds nothing, and is
        discarded. It carries only the short name of the sound — no identifier, no cookie
        and nothing about you — and even that is currently counted by no one.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a play counter is ever switched on, it would record a count per sound and
        nothing that identifies a visitor. This page will be updated before that happens,
        not after.
      </p>
      {/* TODO (owner): the play-count endpoint (functions/api/play.ts) is a Cloudflare
          Pages Function, while the site deploys to GitHub Pages — so it is inert twice
          over: the function is not deployed, and its KV binding (PLAYS) was never
          provisioned. Verified against RESUME.md. If either changes, this paragraph
          and the "Analytics" summary line above must change with it. */}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 4. embeds ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="embeds">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Embedded players on other sites</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      Bleepboard sounds can be embedded in other people’s pages. Here is what that means
      if you met one out in the wild.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Each sound page offers a snippet that puts a small play button on another website
        as an inline frame. If you are reading this because you met one of those buttons
        somewhere else: it is a minimal page containing a play button, the sound’s title
        and a link back here.
      </p>
      <p>
        The embedded player <strong>sets no cookies, saves nothing to your device, loads
        no third-party script and does not track you</strong>. It does not carry the
        favourites feature, and it does not send the play request described above. Your
        browser does fetch the player and the audio file from bleepboard.com, which means
        the request reaches our host in the same way as any other page request, and is
        subject to the same server logs described below.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site the embed appears on is a different matter entirely. That site has its
        own analytics, its own cookies and its own privacy policy, and none of that is
        under our control or covered by this page. The same applies to any advertising it
        runs around the embed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 5. hosting ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="hosting">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Hosting and server logs</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      Somebody has to serve the files, and serving files leaves a record.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Bleepboard is a static site hosted on GitHub Pages. Every page is generated ahead
        of time and published as a plain file — there is no application server, no
        database, and nothing that could hold a user record even if we wanted one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Like every web host, GitHub receives the requests your browser makes and keeps
        standard access logs. These typically include your IP address, the page
        requested, the time, and your browser’s user-agent string. This is how the web
        has worked since the beginning: a server cannot send you a page without knowing
        where to send it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those logs belong to GitHub and are handled under GitHub’s privacy statement. We
        do not have access to them, we cannot query them, and we do not receive any
        report derived from them. That also means we cannot look up, export or delete a
        log entry on request — the honest answer is to point you at the host rather than
        to imply a control we do not have.
      </p>
      {/* TODO (owner): do NOT state a log retention period here. GitHub's retention for
          Pages access logs is theirs to define and change, is not published as a fixed
          figure we can rely on, and is not ours to promise. Leave it described as the
          host's, as above. */}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 6. rights ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="rights">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Your rights and choices</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      Data-protection law gives you a set of rights. Most of them, here, resolve to a
      button in your own browser — which is a good sign, not a dodge.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Because Bleepboard holds no accounts and keeps no user records, there is normally
        nothing on our side to look up, correct, export or erase. The favourites list is
        already yours: it is on your device, we cannot see it, and you can delete it
        yourself in seconds. Advertising identifiers, if advertising is ever running,
        belong to the ad companies and are handled through their opt-outs above.
      </p>

      <h3>EEA and UK (GDPR)</h3>
      <p>
        If you are in the European Economic Area or the United Kingdom, you have the
        right to access the personal data held about you, to have it corrected or erased,
        to restrict or object to processing, and to data portability. You also have the
        right to lodge a complaint with your national data protection authority.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Access, portability, correction and erasure:</strong> we hold no
          personal data to produce, correct or erase. Your favourites are erased by
          clearing site data for bleepboard.com in your browser.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Objection and restriction:</strong> there is no profiling or automated
          decision-making here. If advertising is running, the consent mechanism and the
          opt-outs above are how you object to personalised advertising.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Withdrawing consent:</strong> where consent has been given for
          advertising cookies, you can withdraw it at any time, and it must be as easy to
          withdraw as it was to give.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Server logs</strong> are held by our host, not by us. Requests about
          them are directed to GitHub, whose privacy statement governs them.
        </li>
      </ul>
      {/* TODO (owner): GDPR Art. 13 requires the identity of the controller. This page
          therefore needs, once known and confirmed: the operating legal entity's name;
          a postal address; and — if the site is targeted at or monitors EEA/UK visitors
          without an establishment there — an Art. 27 representative in the EU and/or UK.
          A Data Protection Officer is very unlikely to be required at this scale, but
          that determination is the owner's to make, not ours to assert.
          Nothing is stated above because none of it has been supplied, and an invented
          controller identity is worse than an acknowledged omission. */}

      <h3>California (CCPA/CPRA)</h3>
      <p>
        If you are a California resident, you have the right to know what personal
        information is collected and how it is used, to request deletion, to request
        correction, and to opt out of the “sale” or “sharing” of personal information —
        a definition broad enough to include sharing identifiers with advertising
        partners for cross-context behavioural advertising. You also have the right not
        to be discriminated against for exercising any of these.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Right to know and to delete:</strong> Bleepboard collects no personal
          information through accounts, forms or analytics, so there is no profile to
          disclose or delete. Your favourites are deleted by clearing site data.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sale or sharing:</strong> we do not sell personal information and do
          not share it for cross-context behavioural advertising. If advertising is
          added, ad partners may collect identifiers directly through their own
          technology, which some of these laws treat as “sharing” — the opt-outs above
          are the route to switch that off.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Global Privacy Control:</strong> browser-level opt-out signals are
          honoured by advertising vendors that support them. Since the site sets no
          identifiers itself, there is nothing here for the signal to switch off.
        </li>
      </ul>
      {/* TODO (owner): if/when ads go live and the network's implementation constitutes
          "sharing" under CPRA, a clearly-labelled "Do Not Sell or Share My Personal
          Information" link is required and must appear in the site footer, not only on
          this page. Mediavine and Google both provide a mechanism for this; wire the
          real one rather than writing a link that goes nowhere. */}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 7. children ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="children">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Children</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      Meme sounds skew young. That deserves a straight answer rather than a boilerplate
      paragraph copied off another site.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Bleepboard is a general-audience site rather than one directed at children, but
        we are under no illusions about who plays a soundboard. So: there are no
        accounts, no sign-up, no profiles, no comments, no uploads and no forms. There is
        nowhere on this site for a child — or anyone — to enter a name, an age, an email
        address or a photograph, because no such field exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not knowingly collect personal information from children. If you believe a
        child has somehow provided personal information to us, contact us and we will
        delete it. Realistically the only way that could happen is an email sent to one
        of our addresses, and an email we are asked to delete gets deleted.
      </p>
      <p>
        If advertising is added, ad networks have their own settings for child-directed
        treatment and age-appropriate ad serving, and their own obligations under COPPA
        and similar rules. Those controls sit with the network. We can configure what our
        account offers; we cannot audit what a third party does with a request, and we
        will not claim otherwise.
      </p>
      {/* TODO (owner): before enabling ads, review the network's child-directed /
          "tag for users under the age of consent" settings and decide the site's
          designation deliberately. A soundboard full of Roblox, FNAF, SpongeBob and
          Minecraft audio will draw a young audience regardless of how the site is
          formally classified, and COPPA turns on who the audience actually is. This
          is a judgement call for the owner (ideally with advice), not a default to
          accept silently. */}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ==================== 8. changes & contact ==================== -->
  <section class="section" id="changes">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This policy will change when the site does — most obviously if advertising or a
        play counter is switched on. When that happens, the relevant section is rewritten
        to describe what is actually running, and the date at the top is updated. We would
        rather update this page before a change ships than explain it afterwards.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bleepboard is a static site, so an update takes effect when the site is next
        rebuilt and published, and caches may serve an older copy for a short while after
        that.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="contact">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>How to contact us</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="section-sub">
      A real address, read by a person.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Questions about this policy, or about anything on the site, go to
        <b class="mail">{PRIVACY_TO}</b>. It is the same address used for copyright and
        content reports, and it is read by a person rather than a ticket queue.
      </p>
      {/* TODO (owner): no response-time commitment is stated, and none should be added
          until the owner is willing to hold it. A missed promise on a privacy policy is
          a compliance problem; note that GDPR sets a one-month deadline for responding
          to a data-subject request and CCPA sets 45 days, so any figure published here
          must sit inside those. */}
      {/* TODO (owner): a governing law / jurisdiction clause is deliberately absent —
          it depends on where the operating entity is established, which has not been
          supplied. Add it with the entity details, or leave it out entirely. */}
    </div>

    <p class="fine">
      This page is a plain-English summary of how Bleepboard handles data. It is a
      description of our practices, not legal advice — if you need to know what your
      rights are or how a particular law applies to you, ask a lawyer rather than a
      soundboard.
    </p>

    <p class="btn-row back-row">
      <a class="btn" href="/about/">About Bleepboard</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/dmca/">DMCA &amp; takedowns</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* --- the short version ---------------------------------------------------
     The summary is the only part most people will read, so it gets a panel and
     a two-column ledger rather than another run of prose. The two columns are
     deliberately unequal in length — "what isn't collected" being the longer
     list is the point of the page, and padding the left column to match would
     be dishonest formatting.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .summary {
    max-width: var(--maxw-prose);
    padding: var(--s5);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r);
  }
  .summary-lead {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .ledger {
